WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.200 --> 00:04.700
Emotional well-being plays an important role
in how we face new challenges,

2
00:04.760 --> 00:07.340
and then learn and grow because of them.

3
00:07.400 --> 00:13.020
But when it comes to managing our emotions,
sometimes we're literally stuck in the Stone Age.

4
00:13.080 --> 00:18.460
According to evolutionary biology, we've changed
in many ways over thousands of years,

5
00:18.520 --> 00:25.660
but our brain is still wired to perceive environmental threats
the same way our primitive ancestors did.

6
00:31.320 --> 00:34.140
Our brain perceives danger nonetheless.

7
00:34.200 --> 00:40.088
In fact, our modern-day brain tends to overreact
and lose focus when we sense danger.

8
00:40.148 --> 00:46.328
And it doesn't differentiate all that well between survival threats
and more routine interpersonal conflicts.

9
00:47.748 --> 00:51.288
When we feel positive and calm, we have more perspective

10
00:51.348 --> 00:56.088
and are better able to assess
what is a real threat versus what is not.

11
00:56.148 --> 01:01.688
We can learn from and overcome challenges,
and we're able to connect better with others.

12
01:02.068 --> 01:04.888
Think about what it feels like when you are at your best,

13
01:04.948 --> 01:09.128
learning and performing
and enjoying yourself in the process.

14
01:09.508 --> 01:14.408
We can and should actively replicate this feeling
as often as we can.

15
01:14.468 --> 01:19.848
When we are not feeling this way,
there are a number of ways we can engage and calm our brains

16
01:19.908 --> 01:24.648
to bring us back to this zone of learning,
performance and enjoyment.

17
01:24.708 --> 01:28.148
One of them is the practice of emotional flexibility.

18
01:28.368 --> 01:33.748
Emotional flexibility starts with an awareness
of an emotion that isn't serving you.

19
01:33.808 --> 01:38.948
Think about a time in the past few days
in which you experienced a negative emotion.

20
01:39.008 --> 01:44.628
Maybe you were a little bothered by something,
or maybe you were actually closer to being angry.

21
01:44.688 --> 01:47.428
But bring that moment to mind.

22
01:48.928 --> 01:51.988
What are you feeling and thinking right now?

23
01:52.048 --> 01:56.228
What were you feeling and thinking in the moment? And why?

24
01:58.208 --> 02:03.508
Once you're aware, the next step
to emotional flexibility is counterintuitive.

25
02:04.048 --> 02:06.068
Practice acceptance.

26
02:06.368 --> 02:11.684
It turns out that trying to reject negative feelings
can actually make them stronger.

27
02:11.744 --> 02:17.764
So imagine yourself back in that moment,
and allow yourself to feel however it is you're feeling.

28
02:17.824 --> 02:22.244
There's a reason for it,
so you don't need to interrupt those emotions.

29
02:22.944 --> 02:26.564
Instead, explore your feelings and get curious.

30
02:26.624 --> 02:31.284
What does it feel like? Where does it affect you?

31
02:31.344 --> 02:36.164
Does the feeling seem to have a color? A temperature?

32
02:36.784 --> 02:40.484
You could try to visualize this emotion
that is not serving you.

33
02:40.544 --> 02:42.564
You might even have some fun with it.

34
02:42.624 --> 02:47.124
For example, here comes the blue octopus, my anxiety.

35
02:47.184 --> 02:53.284
With that clear awareness of what's happening to you,
it's easier to recognize and accept the feeling.

36
02:53.344 --> 02:59.124
Once you have accepted your feelings,
you can work through them more quickly by making an active choice.

37
02:59.184 --> 03:00.861
You have two options.

38
03:00.921 --> 03:05.581
Directly addressing what you're feeling,
or choosing to let it go.

39
03:06.281 --> 03:10.061
For example, after a tough conversation with a colleague,

40
03:10.121 --> 03:15.501
you might choose to follow up and to listen,
and understand more about their perspective.

41
03:15.561 --> 03:18.622
Or to vulnerably share how it affected you.

42
03:18.682 --> 03:22.881
You could also sit with it by yourself and consider…
